INT. SUBURBAN KITCHEN - MORNING

Cereal boxes, a half-finished jigsaw on the counter, a calendar with every square filled.

NINA ZELENKO (8, pyjamas, ponytail askew) kneels on a chair, building a tower out of sugar cubes. She doesn't look up when a MAN comes in through the back door.

He is in his thirties, stubble, suitcase in his hand, a travel-worn grey sweater. He stops just inside the mat. He doesn't speak.

NINA
Mum's at work.

MAN
I know.

NINA
You can't come in if she's not here.

MAN
I'm already in.

She considers this. Places another cube. The tower wobbles and holds.

NINA
Are you the plumber?

MAN
No.

NINA
The man from the gas?

MAN
No.

NINA
You look like a man from the gas.

The Man sets the suitcase on the floor. Slowly. Like putting down something that might go off.

MAN (CONT'D)
Do you remember a photograph? On the fridge. A man with a boat.

NINA
That's Daddy. He's in Australia.

MAN
Do you know his name?

NINA
Daddy.

MAN
His other name.

Nina chews her lip. She sets down a cube, lining it up with great care.

NINA
Tomas.

MAN
(quietly)
Tomas.

NINA
Zelenko. Like me.

The Man breathes out. He crosses to the table and pulls out the chair opposite her. Sits. He is eye level with the tower.

NINA (CONT'D)
You have the same nose.

TOMAS
I do.

NINA
You're not in Australia.

TOMAS
Not today.

Nina looks at him then, for the first time, properly. Her eyes trace his jaw, his hands, his collar. She picks up a sugar cube and offers it across the table.

NINA
You can add one. Carefully.

He takes it. His fingers tremble. He lowers it on to the very top of the tower. It holds.

NINA (CONT'D)
(almost whispering)
Daddy.

TOMAS
Nina.

NINA
Mum says you're not coming back.

TOMAS
Mum might be right.

NINA
But you're here.

TOMAS
I'm here this morning.

She nods. Considers. Reaches for another cube.

NINA
Then let's build it taller.

FADE OUT.
